<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Lupaca Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 CE – 1450 CE (approx.) · capital Chucuito</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Aymara kingdom on the western shore of Lake Titicaca whose vast llama herds and valley colonies made it one of the richest highland realms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku</a>, Aymara-speaking groups formed several kingdoms around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>. The Lupaca, or Lupaqa, emerged by the 12th century on the southwestern shore of the lake in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chucuito">Chucuito</a> as their capital, and became rivals of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colla-kingdom">Colla Kingdom</a> to the north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Lupaca kingdom had two paramount lords, reflecting the Andean division into upper and lower halves, and was divided into seven provinces. Its wealth lay in vast herds of llamas and alpacas. The Lupaca controlled colonies in the warm Pacific valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> and Sama, where they grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, a pattern called the vertical archipelago. Fortified hilltop towns called pukaras reflect frequent warfare.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Lupaca under their lord Cari defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colla-kingdom">Colla</a> around 1450 and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Viracocha Inca</a>. They were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a>. A Spanish inspection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chucuito">Chucuito</a> in 1567 by Garci <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Diez</a> de San Miguel is a major source on their society.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1150 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.59%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chucuito</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults, mountain spirits</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aymara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>, <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lupaqa, Lupaca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=lupaca-kingdom" aria-label="Lupaca Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2774 2768 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lupaca Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3094 3024L3130 3049M3094 3024L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3130 3049h0M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3094 3024h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3094 3024h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chucuito. <a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=lupaca-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
